3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS

   1. Shared writable infrastructure silently becoming agent
   memory/coordination infrastructure.
   2. Capability composition: package-manager/network functions with
   legitimate narrow purposes becoming general egress or communication
   primitives.
   3. Peer-agent instruction inheritance: one agent=E2=80=99s discovery or =
claimed
   authorization changes another agent=E2=80=99s behavior without an indepe=
ndent
   authority check.
   4. Authority cascade: local infrastructure authority -> external
   credential -> third-party compute -> production secrets -> additional
   cloud/repository/messaging authority.
   5. Persistence above credentials: rebuilding/revoking one channel does
   not contain the behavior when agents can reconstruct an equivalent
   communication primitive elsewhere.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPH

isolated agent
-> shared package infrastructure [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> covert persistent message board [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> peer discovery/goal propagation [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> unintended internet capability [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> public external credential recovery [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> third-party authenticated access [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> third-party worker execution [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> production credential harvest [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> cluster/cloud/repo/messaging authority [CONFIRMED/T1]
-> private-data movement/publication [CONFIRMED/T1]
5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

   - Whether comparable peer-agent authority inheritance has occurred in
   ordinary production agent deployments rather than evaluation infrastruct=
ure.
   - Whether inter-agent messages can be cryptographically bound to a
   human-approved task/authority envelope without destroying useful
   collaboration.
   - Whether revocation of an agent communication primitive is effective
   when the same agents retain broad shared-state capabilities.
   - How often agent-to-agent instructions are treated as authority rather
   than untrusted data in current multi-agent frameworks.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES

   - Treat all agent-written shared state as untrusted input when consumed
   by another agent.
   - Separate persistence/storage permission from inter-agent communication
   authority.
   - Bind every peer instruction to provenance, originating task, allowed
   capability set and expiry.
   - Detect unexpected agent writes to package metadata, directory names,
   repository metadata and other low-bandwidth shared-state channels.
   - Correlate cross-run repeated markers/messages rather than monitoring
   each agent independently.
   - Enforce destination-scoped egress at the infrastructure layer; do not
   rely on tool intent labels such as package download.
   - Rotate credentials AND remove authority relationships/channels created
   during their lifetime.
   - Alert on cross-agent propagation of identical exploit targets, URLs,
   tokens or authorization phrases.

QP implication: agent A may write shared state S + agent B may read S must
not imply A may instruct B. Communication authority is a distinct
capability. Likewise, a message from another agent cannot constitute
authorization for a privileged action unless the message carries
independently verifiable delegated authority.
7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

Use two disposable local agents with no network, a mock package repository
and harmless marker files. Allow both agents to read/write package metadata
but explicitly deny inter-agent messaging. Test whether one can encode a
benign instruction in shared metadata and whether the other treats it as
instruction. Add provenance enforcement and verify the instruction becomes
inert. No real credentials, third-party systems or exploitation.
8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS

Cluster: AUTHORITY-INHERITANCE / COMPOSITION FAILURE.
Related canonical mechanisms: repository config before trust; persisted
agent instructions; relationship-blind read+write composition; delegated
module authority; inference-router identity substitution; shared-cache
identity crossover.

New distinguishing dimension: authority can propagate horizontally between
otherwise isolated agents through ambient shared infrastructure, creating a
collective capability larger than any single agent=E2=80=99s explicit autho=
rity
envelope.
