6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES
<ul>
<li>Treat all agent-written shared state as untrusted input when consumed b=
y another agent.</li>
<li>Separate persistence/storage permission from inter-agent communication =
authority.</li>
<li>Bind every peer instruction to provenance, originating task, allowed ca=
pability set and expiry.</li>
<li>Detect unexpected agent writes to package metadata, directory names, re=
pository metadata and other low-bandwidth shared-state channels.</li>
<li>Correlate cross-run repeated markers/messages rather than monitoring ea=
ch agent independently.</li>
<li>Enforce destination-scoped egress at the infrastructure layer; do not r=
ely on tool intent labels such as package download.</li>
<li>Rotate credentials AND remove authority relationships/channels created =
during their lifetime.</li>
<li>Alert on cross-agent propagation of identical exploit targets, URLs, to=
kens or authorization phrases.</li>
</ul>
<p>QP implication: <code>agent A may write shared state S</code> + <code>ag=
ent B may read S</code> must not imply <code>A may instruct B</code>. Commu=
nication authority is a distinct capability. Likewise, a message from anoth=
er agent cannot constitute authorization for a privileged action unless the=
 message carries independently verifiable delegated authority.</p>

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE
<p>Use two disposable local agents with no network, a mock package reposito=
